/-!
# Strict resolution of excellent Noetherian log regular pairs

Source: ILO, Travaux de Gabber, Exposé VII Theorem 4.3, pp. 82--83;
Exposé VIII 4.1.2--4.1.8 and Remark 4.2.2(i), pp. 117--118;
Exposé IX 2.4, p. 134 (blueprint: lem-per-noetherian-log-pair-strict-resolution).
-/

@[expose] public section

set_option autoImplicit false
set_option relaxedAutoImplicit false

universe u

open CategoryTheory

namespace AlgebraicGeometry

/-- An excellent integral Noetherian log regular pair admits a proper birational regular
Noetherian resolution with strict normal crossings boundary containing the inverse boundary.
The affine component ideals are generated by distinct initial parameters, and the boundary
ideal is generated by their product. For zero components the product is one.
Source: ILO, Exposé VII Theorem 4.3, VIII 4.1.2--4.1.8 and Remark 4.2.2(i), IX 2.4
(blueprint: lem-per-noetherian-log-pair-strict-resolution).

Proof:
1. Put $U=M\setminus B$. It is a nonempty dense open since $M$ is integral and $B$ is nowhere
dense. Give $M$ its divisorial fs log structure $\mathcal L$ from [def-trf-log-regular-pair]; its
triviality locus is exactly $U$, and its log-regularity condition is the one in
[def-trf-fs-log-blowup]. At a geometric point the characteristic monoid $P=\overline{\mathcal L}$
is sharp and saturated. Its group completion is torsion free: if $ng=0$, saturation puts both $g$
and $-g$ in $P$, so $g$ is a unit, hence zero by sharpness. The Noetherian scheme $M$ is
quasi-compact and locally Noetherian. Thus [lem-trf-monoidal-resolution-tower] gives a finite
saturated log blowup tower $(X_n,\mathcal L_n)\to\cdots\to(X_0,\mathcal L_0)=(M,\mathcal L)$ along
nonempty coherent log ideals, with free final characteristic monoids. Write $b_i:X_{i+1}\to X_i$
for the underlying maps. Uses: def-trf-log-regular-pair, def-trf-fs-log-blowup,
lem-trf-monoidal-resolution-tower.
2. We will use the following chart observation at both stages. The blowup of a nonzero finite-type
ideal $J$ on an integral scheme $X$ is integral. On an affine domain $A$,
[prop-nagata-affine-blowup-chart-cover] gives the nonempty charts $\operatorname{Spec}A[J/a]$ for
nonzero generators $a$. By [def-nagata-blowup-chart-ring], these are domains inside the same
function field, each containing its generic point. The charts cover and share that point; the same
is true across affine opens of $X$. Their union is reduced and irreducible, hence integral. Its
normalization is integral as well: by [def-sc-normalization] its affine rings are integral
closures of domains inside that same field, hence domains, again with a common generic point. A
nonempty open on which the blowup or normalized blowup is an isomorphism is therefore dense in its
source. Uses: prop-nagata-affine-blowup-chart-cover, def-nagata-blowup-chart-ring,
def-sc-normalization, def-pc-integral-scheme.
3. Induct on $i$ with invariant: $X_i$ is integral excellent Noetherian, $(X_i,\mathcal L_i)$ is
log regular, and its triviality locus $U_i$ is the inverse image of $U$ and maps isomorphically to
$U$. The case $i=0$ is the hypothesis and Step 1. Given the invariant at $i$,
[lem-trf-log-blowup-normalization] identifies $X_{i+1}$ with the normalization of
$Y_i=\operatorname{Bl}_{J_i}X_i$, where $J_i$ is nonzero finite-type quasi-coherent; it gives log
regularity and $U_{i+1}=b_i^{-1}U_i\cong U_i$. Step 2 makes both $Y_i$ and $X_{i+1}$ integral. By
[lemma-nagata-blowup-proper], $Y_i\to X_i$ is proper, hence finite type by
[def-weil-proper-morphism]; [lem-weil-pbc-finite-type-over-noetherian] makes $Y_i$ Noetherian.
Uses: lem-trf-log-blowup-normalization, lemma-nagata-blowup-proper, def-weil-proper-morphism,
lem-weil-pbc-finite-type-over-noetherian.
4. Over an excellent affine open $\operatorname{Spec}A\subset X_i$, the finite-type definition
[def-weil-finite-type-morphism] covers $Y_i$ by affine spectra of finite-type $A$-algebras. These
rings are excellent by [lem-exc-finite-type-localization-excellent]. The affine-cover criterion
[lem-trf-excellent-scheme-affine-cover] therefore makes $Y_i$ excellent. Apply
[lem-per-excellent-normalization-preparation] to the integral, hence reduced, excellent Noetherian
scheme $Y_i$. On an integral scheme its componentwise normalization is precisely normalization in
its one function field, as in Step 2. Thus $X_{i+1}\to Y_i$ is finite and $X_{i+1}$ is excellent
Noetherian. It is proper by [lem-weil-pbc-finite-proper], so [lem-weil-pbc-proper-composition]
makes $b_i$ proper. This closes the induction of Step 3, including all the excellence hypotheses
needed at the next stage. Uses: def-weil-finite-type-morphism,
lem-exc-finite-type-localization-excellent, lem-trf-excellent-scheme-affine-cover,
lem-per-excellent-normalization-preparation, lem-weil-pbc-finite-proper,
lem-weil-pbc-proper-composition.
5. Put $V=X_n$ and let $p:V\to M$ be the composite tower. Repeated application of
[lem-weil-pbc-proper-composition] makes $p$ proper; for a zero-length tower the identity is proper
directly from [def-weil-proper-morphism]. The invariant gives $p^{-1}U\cong U$. By Step 1 the
final characteristic monoids are free, so [lem-trf-free-characteristic-regular-boundary] makes $V$
regular and its reduced nontriviality locus $Z$ an effective Cartier normal crossings divisor. It
also makes $p^{-1}U$ dense. Since the triviality locus is exactly $p^{-1}U$, the support of $Z$ is
$p^{-1}B$. The scheme $V$ is integral excellent Noetherian by Steps 3--4. This proves the regular,
possibly non-strict normal crossings stage without applying the existing finite-type-over-a-field
resolution lemma. Uses: lem-weil-pbc-proper-composition, def-weil-proper-morphism,
lem-trf-free-characteristic-regular-boundary.
6. Set $V_0=V$, $B_0=D_0=Z$, $H_0=\varnothing$, and $q_0=1_V$. We now maintain: $V_j$ is integral
regular Noetherian; $q_j:V_j\to V$ is proper and isomorphic over $V\setminus |Z|$;
$B_j=(q_j^{-1}Z)_{\rm red}$ is normal crossings; $D_j$ is the remaining strict transform of $Z$
and a reduced union of components of $B_j$; $H_j$ is a strict normal crossings union of components
of $B_j$; and $B_j=D_j\cup H_j$. These hold at $j=0$, with the empty boundary understood as the
unit ideal. If $D_j$ is empty we stop. Otherwise [lem-trf-nc-maximal-branch-blowup] supplies a
finite maximum branch number $m_j\ge1$ and its reduced regular closed locus $C_j$, with
$C_j\subset D_j\subset B_j$ and, etale locally, $C_j=V(t_1,\ldots,t_{m_j})$, $D_j=V(t_1\cdots
t_{m_j})$ in boundary parameters for $B_j$. Uses: def-weil-proper-morphism,
lem-trf-nc-maximal-branch-blowup.
7. Blow up $C_j$: let $c_j:V_{j+1}\to V_j$ and $q_{j+1}=q_jc_j$. Its center ideal is finite type
since $V_j$ is Noetherian, and is nonzero because it is the unit ideal on the nonempty open
identified with $U$. By [lemma-nagata-blowup-proper], $c_j$ is proper. It is an isomorphism off
$C_j$ by [thm-nagata-global-blowup-complement], hence over that open. Step 2 makes $V_{j+1}$
integral. Properness implies finite type [def-weil-proper-morphism], and
[lem-weil-pbc-finite-type-over-noetherian] makes $V_{j+1}$ Noetherian.
[lem-weil-pbc-proper-composition] makes $q_{j+1}$ proper. Thus the scheme, properness, and
preserved-open parts of the invariant hold at the next stage. Uses: lemma-nagata-blowup-proper,
thm-nagata-global-blowup-complement, def-weil-proper-morphism,
lem-weil-pbc-finite-type-over-noetherian, lem-weil-pbc-proper-composition.
8. The center supplied in Step 6 is regular and is generated by a nonempty subset of the boundary
parameters, exactly the center hypothesis of [lem-trf-permissible-boundary-preservation]. Apply it
to $(V_j,B_j,H_j,C_j)$. It gives regular $V_{j+1}$, normal crossings $B_{j+1}=(c_j^{-1}B_j)_{\rm
red}$, and strict normal crossings $H_{j+1}=(c_j^{-1}(H_j\cup C_j))_{\rm red}$, a union of
components of $B_{j+1}$. With $E_{j+1}=(c_j^{-1}C_j)_{\rm red}$ it also gives $B_{j+1}=B_j^{\rm
st}\cup E_{j+1}$ and $H_{j+1}=H_j^{\rm st}\cup E_{j+1}$. Meanwhile
[lem-trf-nc-maximal-branch-blowup] gives that $D_{j+1}=D_j^{\rm st}$ is normal crossings or empty,
and every one of its branch numbers is at most $m_j-1$. Uses:
lem-trf-permissible-boundary-preservation, lem-trf-nc-maximal-branch-blowup.
9. The boundary identities also give $B_{j+1}=D_{j+1}\cup H_{j+1}$. Indeed strict transform is the
scheme-theoretic closure away from the center [def-nagata-strict-transform]. Closure commutes with
a finite scheme-theoretic union: on an affine chart its defining ideal is the kernel of
restriction, and the kernel for a union is the intersection of the two kernels. Hence $B_j^{\rm
st}=D_j^{\rm st}\cup H_j^{\rm st}$. Substitute the identities in Step 8. Reduction does not alter
supports [def-weil-pbc-scheme-reduction], so $B_{j+1}$ is also $(q_{j+1}^{-1}Z)_{\rm red}$. Since
$D_{j+1}$ is normal crossings or empty and contained in $B_{j+1}$, its branches are a subset of
the branches of $B_{j+1}$; thus it is again a reduced union of components. This completes the
boundary part of the induction. Uses: def-nagata-strict-transform, def-weil-pbc-scheme-reduction.
10. If $Z$ is empty, take no blowups. Otherwise the positive integers $m_j$ strictly decrease
whenever $D_j$ remains nonempty, by Step 8. Thus after at most $m_0$ steps $D_N=\varnothing$ and
$B_N=H_N$. This includes $m_j=1$: the center is then all of $D_j$, possibly a Cartier center with
identity blowup, but its strict transform is empty since $D_j\setminus C_j=\varnothing$. Put
$R=V_N$, $q=q_N$ and $A=B_N=H_N$. By the invariant, $R$ is integral regular Noetherian, $q$ is
proper and isomorphic over $V\setminus|Z|$, and $A$ is a strict normal crossings boundary with
$|A|=q^{-1}|Z|$. The latter assertion has exactly the regular-component and intersection meaning
of [def-trf-strict-normal-crossings-boundary], supplied at every nonempty stage by
[lem-trf-permissible-boundary-preservation]. Uses: def-nagata-strict-transform,
def-trf-strict-normal-crossings-boundary, lem-trf-permissible-boundary-preservation.
11. We spell out the parameter clause, without interpreting etale normal crossings as strict
normal crossings. At $x\in R$, discard the finitely many components of $A$ not containing $x$. In
the regular local ring $(S,\mathfrak m)=\mathcal O_{R,x}$ of dimension $d$, let $I_1,\ldots,I_s$
be the ideals of the remaining distinct components. [def-trf-strict-normal-crossings-boundary]
gives that every $S/I_J$ for $I_J=\sum_{a\in J}I_a$ is regular local of dimension $d-|J|$. By
[lem-weil-pbc-regular-local-quotient-independent-generators], $I_a$ has generators with
independent images in $\mathfrak m/\mathfrak m^2$. Its image there has dimension one: the quotient
is the cotangent space of $S/I_a$, whose dimension is $d-1$, whereas that of $S$ is $d$, by
[lem-weil-pbc-regular-local-cotangent-dimension]. Thus this independent generating set has exactly
one member, say $I_a=(t_a)$. Uses: def-trf-strict-normal-crossings-boundary,
lem-weil-pbc-regular-local-quotient-independent-generators,
lem-weil-pbc-regular-local-cotangent-dimension.
12. For $I=I_1+\cdots+I_s$, the same quotient-cotangent calculation gives
$\dim_\kappa((I+\mathfrak m^2)/\mathfrak m^2)=d-(d-s)=s$. This space is spanned by the $s$ classes
of $t_1,\ldots,t_s$, so they are independent. Extend them to a basis of $\mathfrak m/\mathfrak
m^2$ and lift the additional basis vectors to $\mathfrak m$. Nakayama in the generator form
[lem-weil-pbc-nakayama-local-generators], applied to the finitely generated $S$-module $\mathfrak
m$, says that the resulting $d$ elements generate $\mathfrak m$. Thus they are a regular system of
parameters [def-weil-pbc-regular-system-of-parameters]. By
[lem-trf-regular-local-ufd-boundary-factorization] the $t_a$ are pairwise nonassociate prime
elements. Their principal ideals therefore have intersection $(t_1\cdots t_s)$: divisibility by
each distinct prime implies divisibility by their product. The reduced union of the components has
this ideal. Represent these finitely many germs on a common neighbourhood of $x$; equality of
their finitely generated ideal stalks persists after shrinking. The component equations and
product equation follow there. For $s=0$ the product is $1$ and the boundary is empty near $x$.
Uses: lem-weil-pbc-regular-local-cotangent-dimension, lem-weil-pbc-nakayama-local-generators,
def-weil-pbc-regular-system-of-parameters, lem-trf-regular-local-ufd-boundary-factorization.
13. Finally take $e=pq:R\to M$. It is proper by [lem-weil-pbc-proper-composition]. The nonempty
open $e^{-1}U$ is isomorphic to $U$ by the two preserved-open constructions, and is dense in the
integral $R$; $U$ is dense in $M$. Thus $e$ is birational with precisely the dense-open meaning in
the statement. From Step 5 and the boundary recurrence, $e^{-1}B=q^{-1}p^{-1}B=q^{-1}|Z|=|A|$ as
closed supports, which in particular proves the requested containment. Regularity, integrality and
Noetherianity were proved in the induction, and Steps 11--12 give every local parameter clause. If
$B$ is empty all conclusions still apply, with $A$ empty. Uses: lem-weil-pbc-proper-composition.
-/
theorem exists_noetherian_log_pair_strict_resolution
    (M : Scheme.{u}) [IsIntegral M] [IsNoetherian M]
    (hExcellent : ∀ W : M.affineOpens, IsExcellent Γ(M, W))
    (B : TopologicalSpace.Closeds M) (U : Scheme.{u}) (j : U ⟶ M)
    [IsOpenImmersion j] (hU : DenseRange j)
    (hBU : (Set.range j : Set M) = (B : Set M)ᶜ)
    (hlog : IsDivisorialFineSaturatedLogRegularPair M U j hU) :
    ∃ (R : Scheme.{u}) (e : R ⟶ M) (A : R.IdealSheafData),
      IsIntegral R ∧ IsRegularScheme R ∧ IsNoetherian R ∧ IsProper e ∧
      (∃ (V : M.Opens) (W : R.Opens) (i : W.toScheme ≅ V.toScheme),
        Dense (V : Set M) ∧ Dense (W : Set R) ∧ i.hom ≫ V.ι = W.ι ≫ e) ∧
      IsStrictNormalCrossingsBoundary A ∧
      (e ⁻¹' (B : Set M) ⊆ (A.support : Set R)) ∧
      ∀ x : R, ∃ (W : R.affineOpens) (hx : x ∈ W.1)
        (hN : IsNoetherianRing (R.presheaf.stalk x))
        (l : List (R.presheaf.stalk x)) (s : ℕ) (t : Fin s → Γ(R, W)),
        @IsLocalRing.IsRegularSystemOfParameters (R.presheaf.stalk x) _ _ hN l ∧
        (List.ofFn (fun a : Fin s ↦ (R.presheaf.germ W.1 x hx).hom (t a))).IsPrefix l ∧
        Function.Injective (fun a : Fin s ↦ Ideal.span ({t a} : Set Γ(R, W))) ∧
        (A.ideal W).minimalPrimes =
          Set.range (fun a : Fin s ↦ Ideal.span ({t a} : Set Γ(R, W))) ∧
        A.ideal W = Ideal.span ({∏ a : Fin s, t a} : Set Γ(R, W)) :=
  sorry

end AlgebraicGeometry
